esp32s31: report a position for cyclic receive, and advertise the descriptor limit

Three related defects in the capture path, found while trying to work out why
every microphone recording came back byte-identical.

**The AHB GDMA never reported a position for cyclic receive.** `tx_status()`
had a branch for `cyclic_tx` and branches for the non-cyclic `active_tx` and
`active_rx`, but nothing for `cyclic_rx` - which is the case an ALSA capture
stream always takes. So `dma_set_residue()` was never called and the residue
stayed at the zero `dma_cookie_status()` left, which the dmaengine PCM reads as
"the whole buffer has been filled". The bookkeeping to answer properly was
already there and maintained: receive keeps one descriptor per period and
`rx_node` is the period the engine is filling.

This is why recordings were **deterministic**: with the position pinned at the
end of the buffer, `arecord` was handed the ring's stale contents rather than
anything the DMA had written, so two captures were identical and no change to
the codec's gain could ever alter the result. Every microphone measurement made
before this is void - including the conclusion that the analog front end was at
fault.

**Reporting a position honestly makes the real fault visible**: capture now
overruns continuously instead of returning stale data quietly. That is the
receive clocking problem described in patch 0009, not a regression - a stream
that reports no progress cannot report an overrun either.

**A 4096-byte period was refused at trigger time.** A descriptor carries at
most 4095 bytes and cyclic receive keeps one per period, so the default period
an unconfigured `arecord` asks for came back as -ENOMEM from
`soc_component_trigger`. The engine now advertises `max_seg_size`, and the PCM
caps `period_bytes_max` at 4092 - the largest legal multiple of the 4-byte
frame - so the constraint is negotiated instead of hit.

--- a/drivers/dma/esp32s31-ahb-gdma.c
+++ b/drivers/dma/esp32s31-ahb-gdma.c
@@ -864,6 +864,20 @@
 		dma_set_residue(state, desc->len - chan->tx_node *
 					(desc->len / desc->nperiods));
 	}
+	if (chan->cyclic_rx && chan->cyclic_rx->vd.tx.cookie == cookie) {
+		struct esp32s31_ahb_desc *desc = chan->cyclic_rx;
+
+		/*
+		 * Receive keeps one descriptor per period, so rx_node is the
+		 * period the engine is filling now and everything before it has
+		 * been written.  Without this the residue stays at whatever
+		 * dma_cookie_status left - zero - so the PCM layer reads the
+		 * position as a full buffer and declares an overrun the instant
+		 * capture starts, forever.
+		 */
+		dma_set_residue(state, desc->len - chan->rx_node *
+					(desc->len / desc->nperiods));
+	}
 	if (chan->active_tx && chan->active_tx->vd.tx.cookie == cookie)
 		dma_set_residue(state, chan->active_tx->len);
 	if (chan->active_rx && chan->active_rx->vd.tx.cookie == cookie)
@@ -1087,6 +1101,13 @@
 	dma_dev->src_addr_widths = BIT(DMA_SLAVE_BUSWIDTH_1_BYTE);
 	dma_dev->dst_addr_widths = BIT(DMA_SLAVE_BUSWIDTH_1_BYTE);
 	dma_dev->residue_granularity = DMA_RESIDUE_GRANULARITY_DESCRIPTOR;
+	/*
+	 * One descriptor carries at most 4095 bytes, and cyclic receive keeps
+	 * one descriptor per period.  Advertising the limit lets clients - the
+	 * dmaengine PCM in particular - constrain themselves to it, rather than
+	 * asking for a 4096-byte period and being refused at trigger time.
+	 */
+	dma_set_max_seg_size(dev, AHB_DESC_MAX);
 	dma_dev->device_free_chan_resources = esp32s31_ahb_free_resources;
 	dma_dev->device_prep_dma_memcpy = esp32s31_ahb_prep_memcpy;
 	dma_dev->device_config = esp32s31_ahb_slave_config;
--- a/sound/soc/espressif/esp32s31-i2s.c
+++ b/sound/soc/espressif/esp32s31-i2s.c
@@ -487,7 +487,14 @@
 	.periods_min = 2,
 	.periods_max = ESP32S31_I2S_BUFFER_BYTES / 1024,
 	.period_bytes_min = 1024,
-	.period_bytes_max = 4096,
+	/*
+	 * A GDMA descriptor carries at most 4095 bytes and receive keeps one
+	 * descriptor per period, so a 4096-byte period - which is what an
+	 * unconfigured arecord asks for - is rejected with -ENOMEM from
+	 * soc_component_trigger.  4092 is the largest legal multiple of the
+	 * 4-byte frame.
+	 */
+	.period_bytes_max = 4092,
 	.buffer_bytes_max = ESP32S31_I2S_BUFFER_BYTES,
 };
 
